1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,928
This video is about deterministic finite automata, or D F As.

2
00:00:05,378 --> 00:00:21,378
A D F A is the target language of the regex compiler. We learn to read a D F A, run it, write it as a table, and design one from a small program. At the end we solve the execution questions from the question bank.

3
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:34,777
You specify tokens with regular expressions. But you need code: a lexer that walks the input and cuts it into tokens. So far, a person read the regexes and wrote that code.

4
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:41,222
We can do better. We generate the lexer from the regexes. This is a mini compiler.

5
00:00:41,672 --> 00:00:45,235
The source of this compiler is a regular expression.

6
00:00:45,685 --> 00:00:51,765
The target is a program that walks the input and recognizes it. We call it a recognizer.

7
00:00:52,215 --> 00:00:54,135
The compiler has three steps.

8
00:00:54,585 --> 00:00:57,102
Step 1: regex to N F A.

9
00:00:57,552 --> 00:01:00,005
Step 2: N F A to D F A.

10
00:01:00,455 --> 00:01:03,271
Step 3: D F A to minimal D F A.

11
00:01:03,721 --> 00:01:16,756
The target must not be Java or C. We want a small language for recognizers, easy to execute anywhere. That language is the D F A. This video is about the D F A.

12
00:01:17,206 --> 00:01:28,406
A finite automaton is a machine with finitely many states. It reads an input string and accepts or rejects it. The plural of automaton is automata.

13
00:01:28,856 --> 00:01:40,184
D F A means deterministic finite automaton. For each state and each input letter, there is exactly one next state. So a D F A follows one path.

14
00:01:40,634 --> 00:01:50,042
N F A means nondeterministic finite automaton. One letter may have several next states. Here q zero has two a arrows.

15
00:01:50,492 --> 00:01:55,463
A letter may also have no next state. Here q zero has no b arrow.

16
00:01:55,913 --> 00:02:01,033
An N F A may also move on an epsilon edge, which reads no input.

17
00:02:01,483 --> 00:02:13,877
An N F A accepts if at least one path reads the whole input and ends in an accepting state. N F As are the topic of the next video. Today is about D F As.

18
00:02:14,327 --> 00:02:19,106
A D F A is a tiny programming language. It gets an input string.

19
00:02:19,556 --> 00:02:26,489
It reads one character at a time, and follows the transition for that character. It has no other memory.

20
00:02:26,939 --> 00:02:32,379
At the end of the string, it accepts if its state is accepting. Otherwise it rejects.

21
00:02:32,829 --> 00:02:49,128
D F As are easy to execute and easy to simulate. But they are long to write by hand, so we generate them. The goal: for any regular expression, build an automaton that accepts exactly the strings the regex matches.

22
00:02:49,578 --> 00:02:55,679
Here is the D F A from the slides. The states are the bubbles: q zero to q five.

23
00:02:56,129 --> 00:03:01,143
The initial state is the state with the arrow from nowhere. Here it is q zero.

24
00:03:01,593 --> 00:03:08,995
A transition is an arrow labelled with a letter. Here the arrow from q zero to q one is labelled a.

25
00:03:09,445 --> 00:03:15,461
The letters on the arrows form the alphabet. It is called sigma. Here sigma is a and b.

26
00:03:15,911 --> 00:03:20,669
An accepting state has a double circle. Here only q four accepts.

27
00:03:21,119 --> 00:03:30,505
Deterministic means: in every state, for every letter, exactly one arrow. Look at q two. It has one a arrow and one b arrow.

28
00:03:30,955 --> 00:03:38,699
State q five has a self loop on a and b. You can never leave it, and it does not accept. This is the error state.

29
00:03:39,149 --> 00:03:44,205
Now run the machine. The input is a b a. Pause the video and try it.

30
00:03:44,655 --> 00:03:47,855
Start in q zero, before any letter is read.

31
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:50,865
Read a. q zero goes to q one.

32
00:03:51,315 --> 00:03:53,982
Read b. q one goes to q two.

33
00:03:54,432 --> 00:03:57,824
Read the last a. q two goes to q five.

34
00:03:58,274 --> 00:04:03,970
The input is finished. q five does not accept, so the machine rejects a b a.

35
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:06,319
Now the input a b b a.

36
00:04:06,769 --> 00:04:08,326
Start in q zero.

37
00:04:08,776 --> 00:04:10,483
Read a. Go to q one.

38
00:04:10,933 --> 00:04:12,917
Read b. Go to q two.

39
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:15,521
Read b. Go to q three.

40
00:04:15,971 --> 00:04:17,934
Read a. Go to q four.

41
00:04:18,384 --> 00:04:24,037
The input is finished, and q four accepts. So the machine accepts a b b a.

42
00:04:24,487 --> 00:04:27,794
Take the input a b b. Start in q zero.

43
00:04:28,244 --> 00:04:30,036
Read a, go to q one.

44
00:04:30,486 --> 00:04:32,406
Read b, go to q two.

45
00:04:32,856 --> 00:04:34,861
Read b, go to q three.

46
00:04:35,311 --> 00:04:40,495
The input stops here. q three does not accept, so a b b is rejected.

47
00:04:40,945 --> 00:04:49,905
To accept a string, the machine scans the entire input, and the last state must be accepting. Nothing is decided before the end.

48
00:04:50,355 --> 00:05:04,521
Only q four accepts, and only a b b a ends there. Every other string ends in a state that does not accept. So the language is the set with only a b b a, the regex a b b a.

49
00:05:04,971 --> 00:05:16,085
The error state is not part of the definition. q five is an ordinary state. Error is only our name for a state that you can never leave and never accept from.

50
00:05:16,535 --> 00:05:25,709
The course notes show the same idea on a smaller machine. Machine 1 accepts only the string a b. The alphabet is a and b.

51
00:05:26,159 --> 00:05:30,105
But some arrows are missing. State 1 has no b arrow.

52
00:05:30,555 --> 00:05:32,454
State 2 has no a arrow.

53
00:05:32,904 --> 00:05:35,165
State 3 has no arrows at all.

54
00:05:35,615 --> 00:05:44,981
There is an implicit error state. Call it X. Add an arrow to X for every letter that has no arrow. X loops to itself on every letter.

55
00:05:45,431 --> 00:05:49,249
Run a b. The path is 1, 2, 3, and 3 accepts.

56
00:05:49,699 --> 00:05:54,478
Run a. The path is 1, 2. The input ends in 2, so reject.

57
00:05:54,928 --> 00:05:58,235
Run a a. The path is 1, 2, X. Reject.

58
00:05:58,685 --> 00:06:05,831
Run a a b b. The path is 1, 2, X, X, X. X keeps the machine in X. Reject.

59
00:06:06,281 --> 00:06:12,383
A missing arrow means: go to the error state. The notes usually leave X out of the drawing.

60
00:06:12,833 --> 00:06:17,185
Machine 2 from the notes. State 1 is the start, and it accepts.

61
00:06:17,635 --> 00:06:19,320
a goes from 1 to 2.

62
00:06:19,770 --> 00:06:21,690
b goes from 2 back to 1.

63
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:24,785
Run a b a. Start in state 1.

64
00:06:25,235 --> 00:06:27,262
Read a. Go to state 2.

65
00:06:27,712 --> 00:06:33,536
Read b. Go back to state 1. State 1 accepts. But the input is not finished.

66
00:06:33,986 --> 00:06:36,674
Read the last a. Go to state 2.

67
00:06:37,124 --> 00:06:42,969
Now the input is finished, and state 2 does not accept. So a b a is rejected.

68
00:06:43,419 --> 00:06:59,099
The path passed through an accepting state, but that does not count. The question is only this: at the end of the input, is the machine in an accepting state? Machine 2 accepts the empty string, a b, a b a b, and so on.

69
00:06:59,549 --> 00:07:12,968
What kind of program is an automaton? Suppose you write it as a program. The input is a string. The program reads one character at a time. At the end of the file, it says accept or reject.

70
00:07:13,418 --> 00:07:19,050
A state of the automaton is the line number plus the values of all the variables.

71
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:24,535
A transition is: read one character, run the line, and go to the next line.

72
00:07:24,985 --> 00:07:29,315
The end of the input is the decision: is the state double circled?

73
00:07:29,765 --> 00:07:41,349
This gives a design method. To get an automaton, write the program under these restrictions. Then compile it in your head. The automaton is the state space of the program.

74
00:07:41,799 --> 00:07:48,562
Which program features compile into an automaton? Loops compile: a transition can go backwards.

75
00:07:49,012 --> 00:07:55,071
Recursion does not compile: each call gets fresh local variables, and that is counting.

76
00:07:55,521 --> 00:07:58,230
If and switch on the next character compile.

77
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:03,203
Dynamic memory and unbounded data structures do not compile.

78
00:08:03,653 --> 00:08:07,279
Finitely many variables of finite size compile.

79
00:08:07,729 --> 00:08:15,153
Nondeterminism does not compile into a real program, because real programs are deterministic.

80
00:08:15,603 --> 00:08:27,785
Count the states of a program like this. Take five lines and two boolean variables, b 1 and b 2. A state is a line number plus the values of b 1 and b 2.

81
00:08:28,235 --> 00:08:30,368
Five lines give five columns.

82
00:08:30,818 --> 00:08:40,290
Two booleans have 2 times 2, which is 4, value pairs: false false, false true, true false, and true true. So there are four rows.

83
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:49,167
The grid has 5 times 4, which is 20, cells. So the program has 20 states. The number is large, but it is finite.

84
00:08:49,617 --> 00:08:56,742
An exercise from the slides. L is the set of strings over a and b with as many a letters as b letters.

85
00:08:57,192 --> 00:09:06,152
Write a program that recognizes L. Then check if every gadget you used compiles to an automaton. Pause the video and try it.

86
00:09:06,602 --> 00:09:10,058
Here is a program. A counter c starts at zero.

87
00:09:10,508 --> 00:09:12,044
Each a adds one.

88
00:09:12,494 --> 00:09:14,329
Each b subtracts one.

89
00:09:14,779 --> 00:09:17,168
At the end, accept if c is zero.

90
00:09:17,618 --> 00:09:22,098
The counter is the problem. Each value of c is a different state.

91
00:09:22,548 --> 00:09:30,399
What is the width of the counter? 32 bits? 64 bits? The input has no bound, so no width is enough.

92
00:09:30,849 --> 00:09:36,545
We need arbitrary precision, and so memory on demand. That is not finite.

93
00:09:36,995 --> 00:09:48,600
This is not a proof. We tried one program and it failed. The pumping lemma gives the proof. But it is the method: write the program, check the gadgets, and count the states.

94
00:09:49,050 --> 00:09:54,277
The same machine, with three accepting states: q zero, q two and q four.

95
00:09:54,727 --> 00:09:58,353
Which strings does it accept? Pause the video and try it.

96
00:09:58,803 --> 00:10:08,041
q zero is the initial state, and it accepts. So the empty string is accepted. The machine reads nothing and ends in q zero.

97
00:10:08,491 --> 00:10:12,885
a b goes q zero, q one, q two. q two accepts.

98
00:10:13,335 --> 00:10:16,173
a b b a goes to q four, which accepts.

99
00:10:16,623 --> 00:10:24,388
Every other string ends in a state that does not accept. So L is the set: epsilon, a b, a b b a.

100
00:10:24,838 --> 00:10:34,715
Epsilon and lambda both mean the empty string. The notes use epsilon. Some books use lambda. Epsilon is not a letter of the alphabet.

101
00:10:35,165 --> 00:10:44,232
Change this automaton so that its language is infinite. What must you add? What must you also change? Pause the video and try it.

102
00:10:44,682 --> 00:10:54,687
A finite machine can accept infinitely many strings only with a cycle on an accepting path. For example, add a self loop on a at q one.

103
00:10:55,137 --> 00:11:02,625
But q one already has an a arrow, to q five. Now q one has two a arrows. That breaks determinism.

104
00:11:03,075 --> 00:11:10,030
So the old a arrow from q one to q five must go. Again there is one arrow per letter per state.

105
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:17,136
Now a a b is accepted. It goes q zero, q one, then around the loop to q one, then q two.

106
00:11:17,586 --> 00:11:34,354
The new language is epsilon, a b, a b b a, a a b, a a a b, and more. As a regex: epsilon, or a plus, b, then b a optional. A python check compared the machine and this regex on all strings up to length 9.

107
00:11:34,804 --> 00:11:40,372
Two more machines. What is the language of the first one? Pause the video and try it.

108
00:11:40,822 --> 00:11:42,699
q zero loops on a.

109
00:11:43,149 --> 00:11:46,093
Then one b goes to q one, which accepts.

110
00:11:46,543 --> 00:11:49,637
Any more letters go to q two, the error state.

111
00:11:50,087 --> 00:11:54,673
So the language is a star b: any number of a letters, then one b.

112
00:11:55,123 --> 00:12:04,297
The second machine has the alphabet with only the letter 1. Each 1 moves between q zero and q one. Only q zero accepts.

113
00:12:04,747 --> 00:12:19,787
So it accepts the strings of even length: epsilon, 1 1, 1 1 1 1, and so on. For example, 1 1 goes q zero, q one, q zero. With one letter, the length is the only thing a string can encode.

114
00:12:20,237 --> 00:12:26,893
Now the strings whose length is divisible by 3. Or by 7. How do you build these machines?

115
00:12:27,343 --> 00:12:31,545
Think as a programmer. Keep a counter n. It starts at zero.

116
00:12:31,995 --> 00:12:34,747
Each letter adds one, modulo 3.

117
00:12:35,197 --> 00:12:37,587
At the end, accept if n is zero.

118
00:12:38,037 --> 00:12:43,306
The counter has three values: 0, 1 and 2. Each value is one state.

119
00:12:43,756 --> 00:12:49,623
Each 1 moves to the next remainder: 0 to 1, 1 to 2, and 2 back to 0.

120
00:12:50,073 --> 00:12:52,291
Only remainder zero accepts.

121
00:12:52,741 --> 00:13:03,365
In general, a counter mod n needs n states, one per remainder. For 7, use 7 states in a ring. This counter is finite, so it compiles.

122
00:13:03,815 --> 00:13:13,863
A program cannot generate a picture. So we also write a D F A as a tuple of five parts: Q, sigma, delta, q zero, and F.

123
00:13:14,313 --> 00:13:18,111
Q is the set of states, here q zero to q five.

124
00:13:18,561 --> 00:13:23,275
Sigma is the alphabet, here a and b. Epsilon is not in sigma.

125
00:13:23,725 --> 00:13:27,245
Delta is the transition function: all the arrows.

126
00:13:27,695 --> 00:13:30,149
q zero is the initial state.

127
00:13:30,599 --> 00:13:36,849
F is the set of accepting states. Here F has only q four. F is a subset of Q.

128
00:13:37,299 --> 00:13:45,427
Delta maps a state and a letter to one state. So delta is a table, with one row per state and one column per letter.

129
00:13:45,877 --> 00:13:50,208
Row q zero. On a, go to q one. On b, go to q five.

130
00:13:50,658 --> 00:13:54,861
Row q one. On a, go to q five. On b, go to q two.

131
00:13:55,311 --> 00:13:59,663
Row q two. On a, go to q five. On b, go to q three.

132
00:14:00,113 --> 00:14:04,507
Row q three. On a, go to q four. On b, go to q five.

133
00:14:04,957 --> 00:14:08,221
Row q four. Both letters go to q five.

134
00:14:08,671 --> 00:14:15,242
Row q five. Both letters stay in q five. The table is the whole machine, written down.

135
00:14:15,692 --> 00:14:24,524
Determinism means that every cell of the table has exactly one state in it. No cell is empty, and no cell has two states.

136
00:14:24,974 --> 00:14:31,267
Now run a D F A formally. One step is delta of q and x. It gives the next state.

137
00:14:31,717 --> 00:14:51,216
For a whole string we use delta star. It applies delta letter by letter. Delta star of q and the empty string is q. Delta star of q and x w is delta star of delta of q and x, and w. So: take one step on the first letter, then continue with the rest.

138
00:14:51,666 --> 00:14:54,994
For example, delta star of q zero and a b.

139
00:14:55,444 --> 00:15:02,783
The first letter is a. Delta of q zero and a is q one. So we get delta star of q one and b.

140
00:15:03,233 --> 00:15:10,038
Delta of q one and b is q two. The rest is the empty string, so the answer is q two.

141
00:15:10,488 --> 00:15:24,227
A language over sigma is any set of strings. The language of M is every string w where delta star of q zero and w is in F. The rejected strings are the same with not in F.

142
00:15:24,677 --> 00:15:34,746
Some small machines over a and b. One state that does not accept, with a loop on both letters. Nothing is accepted. This is the empty language.

143
00:15:35,196 --> 00:15:42,001
The same machine, but the state accepts. Every string is accepted. This is sigma star, all strings.

144
00:15:42,451 --> 00:15:49,726
Two states. The start accepts, and any letter goes to an error state. Only the empty string is accepted.

145
00:15:50,176 --> 00:15:56,789
Strings that start with a b. a then b reaches q two, which accepts and loops on both letters.

146
00:15:57,239 --> 00:16:02,231
A wrong first letter or a wrong second letter goes to q three, the error state.

147
00:16:02,681 --> 00:16:07,289
Now the strings that do not start with a b. Pause the video and try it.

148
00:16:07,739 --> 00:16:13,158
Use the machine for starts with a b. Every string ends in exactly one state.

149
00:16:13,608 --> 00:16:20,328
Swap the accepting states. q zero, q one and q three now accept, and q two does not.

150
00:16:20,778 --> 00:16:31,061
Look at the short strings. The empty string ends in q zero. a ends in q one. b ends in q three. None of them starts with a b.

151
00:16:31,511 --> 00:16:36,908
So q zero and q one must accept too, not only the error state q three.

152
00:16:37,358 --> 00:16:44,569
An exercise. The alphabet is 0 and 1. Accept the strings that contain 0 0 1.

153
00:16:45,019 --> 00:16:53,125
For example, 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 is accepted. It contains 0 0 1.

154
00:16:53,575 --> 00:17:00,551
1 1 1 0 1 0 1 1 is rejected. It has no 0 0 1 anywhere.

155
00:17:01,001 --> 00:17:15,209
Think like a programmer. What must you remember as you read the input? You cannot call String contains. Use boolean variables and small counters only. Then compile the program. Pause the video and try it.

156
00:17:15,659 --> 00:17:22,465
What must we remember? Only how many zeros in a row we have seen. Keep that number in a counter z.

157
00:17:22,915 --> 00:17:32,643
The count is 0, 1 or 2. Each value is one state. Call them epsilon, 0, and 0 0, for the zeros seen so far.

158
00:17:33,093 --> 00:17:39,642
A 0 increments the counter. Epsilon goes to 0, and 0 goes to 0 0.

159
00:17:40,092 --> 00:17:47,815
More than two zeros is the same as two: the counter saturates. So 0 0 loops to itself on 0.

160
00:17:48,265 --> 00:17:53,406
A 1 after fewer than two zeros resets the counter to the state epsilon.

161
00:17:53,856 --> 00:18:00,917
A 1 after two zeros completes 0 0 1. Go to the accepting state 0 0 1.

162
00:18:01,367 --> 00:18:19,031
After that, the answer cannot change. The accepting state loops on 0 and 1, and eats the rest of the input. A python check ran the program and this D F A on all strings up to length 10. They agree. The program has exactly four states.

163
00:18:19,481 --> 00:18:25,753
Run the machine on 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0. Start in epsilon.

164
00:18:26,203 --> 00:18:28,593
Read 0. Go to state 0.

165
00:18:29,043 --> 00:18:33,523
Read 1. Only one zero was seen, so reset to epsilon.

166
00:18:33,973 --> 00:18:36,362
Read 0. Go to state 0.

167
00:18:36,812 --> 00:18:39,607
Read 0. Go to state 0 0.

168
00:18:40,057 --> 00:18:45,731
Read 0. Three zeros in a row is the same as two. Stay in 0 0.

169
00:18:46,181 --> 00:18:51,067
Read 1. That completes 0 0 1. Go to the accepting state.

170
00:18:51,517 --> 00:18:54,461
Read 0. Stay in 0 0 1.

171
00:18:54,911 --> 00:18:58,303
Read the last 0. Stay in 0 0 1.

172
00:18:58,753 --> 00:19:06,091
The input is finished, and the state 0 0 1 accepts. So the string contains 0 0 1.

173
00:19:06,541 --> 00:19:12,088
The complement of a D F A keeps the states, the alphabet, the table and the start state.

174
00:19:12,538 --> 00:19:17,786
Only the accepting states change: the new accepting set is Q minus F.

175
00:19:18,236 --> 00:19:22,033
The new language is all strings minus the old language.

176
00:19:22,483 --> 00:19:33,321
So, does not contain 0 0 1: swap the accepting states. Epsilon, 0 and 0 0 now accept, and 0 0 1 does not.

177
00:19:33,771 --> 00:19:46,613
This works only because delta is total. Every string lands in exactly one state. That state is either in F or not in F. So think of the positive case, build it, then swap.

178
00:19:47,063 --> 00:19:54,914
Be careful with a drawing that leaves the error state implicit. Take machine 1 from the notes. It accepts only a b.

179
00:19:55,364 --> 00:20:01,465
Swap its accepting states without drawing X. Now 1 and 2 accept, and 3 does not.

180
00:20:01,915 --> 00:20:08,571
Run a a. From 2 there is no a arrow, so the machine goes to the implicit error state and rejects.

181
00:20:09,021 --> 00:20:14,909
But a a is not a b, so the complement must accept it. The swap gave a wrong machine.

182
00:20:15,359 --> 00:20:21,141
First draw the error state X with all its arrows. Then swap. Now X accepts.

183
00:20:21,591 --> 00:20:31,319
Run a a again. The path is 1, 2, X, and X accepts. A python check confirms that this machine accepts every string except a b.

184
00:20:31,769 --> 00:20:37,635
Now the execution questions from the question bank. The alphabet is a, b and c.

185
00:20:38,085 --> 00:20:39,792
State 1 is the start.

186
00:20:40,242 --> 00:20:41,735
State 2 accepts.

187
00:20:42,185 --> 00:20:44,319
a or c goes from 1 to 2.

188
00:20:44,769 --> 00:20:46,689
b goes from 2 back to 1.

189
00:20:47,139 --> 00:20:53,731
Run the machine on a b c, on a b, and on a c b. For each, give the states and the output.

190
00:20:54,181 --> 00:20:55,823
Pause the video and try it.

191
00:20:56,273 --> 00:20:59,089
Input a b c. Start in state 1.

192
00:20:59,539 --> 00:21:01,587
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

193
00:21:02,037 --> 00:21:04,469
Read b. Go from 2 back to 1.

194
00:21:04,919 --> 00:21:07,117
Read c. Go from 1 to 2.

195
00:21:07,567 --> 00:21:12,004
The input is finished in state 2, which accepts. Output: accept.

196
00:21:12,454 --> 00:21:15,099
Input a b. Start in state 1.

197
00:21:15,549 --> 00:21:17,597
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

198
00:21:18,047 --> 00:21:20,479
Read b. Go from 2 back to 1.

199
00:21:20,929 --> 00:21:27,009
The input ends in state 1. State 1 does not accept, so the output is reject.

200
00:21:27,459 --> 00:21:35,929
Input a c b. Here we need the error state. State 2 has no arrow for c. So we draw the implicit error state X.

201
00:21:36,379 --> 00:21:38,021
Start in state 1.

202
00:21:38,471 --> 00:21:40,519
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

203
00:21:40,969 --> 00:21:44,895
Read c. State 2 has no c arrow, so go to X.

204
00:21:45,345 --> 00:21:47,585
Read b. X stays in X.

205
00:21:48,035 --> 00:21:55,971
The input ends in X, so the output is reject. This matches the posted solution: 2 to X, then X to X.

206
00:21:56,421 --> 00:22:03,397
The question bank has a second machine. The b edge is replaced by an epsilon edge from 2 back to 1.

207
00:22:03,847 --> 00:22:06,556
Run it on a c. Start in state 1.

208
00:22:07,006 --> 00:22:09,054
Read a. Go from 1 to 2.

209
00:22:09,504 --> 00:22:17,931
Take the epsilon edge. Go from 2 back to 1. This reads no input, so the solution writes a dash in the input column.

210
00:22:18,381 --> 00:22:20,578
Read c. Go from 1 to 2.

211
00:22:21,028 --> 00:22:25,103
The input is finished in state 2, so the output is accept.

212
00:22:25,553 --> 00:22:38,075
A machine with an epsilon edge is not a D F A. It is an N F A. This machine accepts every non empty string of a and c. The next video is about N F As.

213
00:22:38,525 --> 00:22:52,008
To summarize. A D F A is a tuple: states, alphabet, transition function, start state, and accepting states. The transition function is a table with exactly one state in every cell.

214
00:22:52,458 --> 00:23:00,010
To run a D F A, follow one arrow per letter. Accept only if the state at the end of the input is accepting.

215
00:23:00,460 --> 00:23:07,543
A missing arrow goes to the implicit error state. Draw the error state when you run a machine or complement it.

216
00:23:07,993 --> 00:23:20,110
To design a D F A, write a program with finite variables. Its state space is the D F A. A counter mod n compiles. An unbounded counter does not.

217
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:32,720
To complement a D F A, keep the table and swap the accepting and non accepting states. The table must be total. Next video: N F As and regular languages.
